  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM EDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis and Forecast:\n\nSfc high pressure slowly drifts over northern Michigan this evening \nin wake of cold advection, repositioning just to the east by \nSaturday morning. Temperatures drop into the mid 20s to low 30s most \nareas as a result. Not a huge signal for fog tonight, but not \nimpossible given the sfc pattern and some progged soundings. Thus \nthere could be some patches over the interior, river valleys, and \nnotorious foggy spots. Otherwise, sfc high continues to shift east \nwith subtle energy embedded within the west to east flow aloft this \nweekend. Warm advection and better moisture flux later Saturday into \nearly Sunday will likely result in few to scattered showers across \nthe area, but likely not amounting to much overall (<0.10\" for \nmany). Temperatures steadily warm a few degrees each day through \nthis weekend. \n\nThe focus for the weather over the next week or so will be the early \nto mid next week time frame. A huge trough, with plenty of individual \npieces of energy, dives into the Intermountain West/West coast. This \nfeature will shift east with time into mid next week and as you'd \nexpect, a potent sfc low pressure system will develop across the \nmiddle portions of the country. The question becomes, how do the \nchaotic pieces of energy interact and thus where does the low \npressure system track/best upper lift reside, and what this all \nmeans for northern Michigan. Well, at this time, it looks like the \nUpper Midwest is the favored area for a first/parent low pressure \nsystem to track mid next week based on the current ensembles, with a \nsecondary area of low pressure system possibly tracking across the \nMississippi Valley. Cannot stress enough how convoluted this pattern \nlooks aloft, but it does appear northern Michigan will get in on at \nleast some rain. Current ensemble means show the potential for a \nhealthy rain (0.5\" to 1.0\") across the area, but the exact \ninteraction of these features will change this forecast for the \nlater Monday-ish to Wednesday-ish time. There could be multiple \nrounds of rain as well, or perhaps not much rain early in the week \nand more mid week. So a lot to iron out in the coming days. Not much \nsensible weather outside of the rain next week except for breezy \nconditions at times.",
